Alli McDermitt
Program 3: Compressing Files
CS3411 Spring 2025

The program works on every case I've tested it on — admittedly, in this busy part of the year, 
I've only been able to test it on the provided materials, but I did so quite thoroughly using
a modification of the Shell script testing suite I built for the last program. It works to encode
and decode all of the test cases, and compresses at the same rates as the provided binaries.

The chart below describes the measured compression ratios for encode, decode, and gzip, in that
order. To keep it within 100 characters of width I shortened certain filenames, but they all
follow a predictable order.

COMP. RATIOS | hello.txt | const[..].txt | FGSA | encode | ->.o | ->.c | decode | ->.o | ->.c |
-------------|-----------|---------------|------|--------|------|------|--------|------|------|
encode       |	 0.45    |     1.00      | 1.17 |  2.04  | 1.67 | 1.01 |  2.21  | 1.74 | 1.01 |
encode-s     |   0.45    |     1.00      | 1.17 |  2.04  | 1.67 | 1.01 |  2.21  | 1.74 | 1.01 |
gzip         |   0.30    |     3.22      | 2.87 |  4.09  | 2.85 | 2.64 |  4.49  | 2.68 | 2.35 |

♥

Addendum for Github: This is a single-file compression system involving two files. "encode" will 
create an encoded version of the file supplied through STDIN, and decode will reconstruct it and 
print it to STDOUT. The compression ratios for the provided test files as well as the files 
associated with the program itself are listed in the above table.

Notably, this program interacts with single-bit increments, and does so quite efficiently, using
each file's included functions.

Like Program 2, this one includes a Shell script, "test.sh", to test basic functionality.
